# Omarchy Child Protect Guardian — execution allowlist for the child (Threat model §4 L2).
# Drop into /etc/fapolicyd/rules.d/50-guardian.rules then: sudo fapolicyd-cli --update && sudo systemctl restart fapolicyd
#
# PURPOSE: stop the child running a downloaded AppImage / static ELF / portable binary from $HOME,
# /tmp or a USB stick. Only files fapolicyd TRUSTS (seeded from pacman-owned files + guardian-approved
# installs — see README.md) may be executed by the child.
#
# Rule grammar is `decision perm subject : object`. Trust must be asserted on the OBJECT (the file
# being executed), NOT on the subject (the calling process) — a trusted shell launching an untrusted
# binary must still be denied. First match wins, top to bottom.
#
# !!! DANGER: fapolicyd in enforcing mode can lock the machine out of running programs. ALWAYS test in
# permissive mode first (README) and keep a root shell open. Replace 1001 with `id -u <child>`.

# 1) Root is unrestricted (updates, guardiand, recovery).
allow perm=any uid=0 : all

# 2) The child may execute a file ONLY if that file (the object) is trusted. Untrusted object → deny.
#    This also stops the loader-laundering trick: `ld-linux.so <untrusted-elf>` opens the untrusted
#    object for execute, which this rule denies.
deny_audit perm=execute uid=1001 : all trust=0
allow      perm=execute uid=1001 : all trust=1

# 3) Deny the child opening untrusted ELF shared libraries for execution-mapping (defence in depth).
deny_audit perm=open uid=1001 : all ftype=application/x-sharedlib trust=0

# 4) Everyone else: allow for now (tighten per-account later).
allow perm=any all : all
